WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:02.836
-We're back with
Jeffrey Wright.

00:00:02.836 --> 00:00:05.104
This is a film that tackles
a lot of very serious issues,

00:00:05.104 --> 00:00:06.906
and a lot of the film
is very serious,

00:00:06.906 --> 00:00:10.209
but it's also a satire,
and it's really funny.

00:00:10.209 --> 00:00:14.213
But playing satire is not the
way you would act in a comedy.

00:00:14.213 --> 00:00:16.549
You have to be both aware of it,
but have to,

00:00:16.549 --> 00:00:18.951
I would say, approach it
with a real sincerity.

00:00:18.951 --> 00:00:23.022
-Well, I think that satire
in our film

00:00:23.022 --> 00:00:26.058
is really tragedy in disguise.
-Yeah. [ Laughs ]

00:00:26.058 --> 00:00:29.228
-Disguised as comedy, you know?

00:00:29.228 --> 00:00:31.998
The film, you know,
we're a small film.

00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:34.901
We shot in 26 days.

00:00:34.901 --> 00:00:36.802
I think the budget of our film

00:00:36.802 --> 00:00:40.406
was probably the catering budget
for the Batman movie.

00:00:40.406 --> 00:00:42.575
-Yeah, yeah.

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:44.077
-But we're punching
above our weight,

00:00:44.077 --> 00:00:47.079
and we really loved this script.

00:00:47.079 --> 00:00:50.784
It's a small movie
with big ideas.

00:00:50.784 --> 00:00:54.254
And these are conversations
that we're having in the film

00:00:54.254 --> 00:00:57.156
that we're having across
our country right now,

00:00:57.156 --> 00:01:00.759
conversations around race and
"ooh, scary."

00:01:00.759 --> 00:01:01.894
-Yeah.
-Right?

00:01:01.894 --> 00:01:06.065
And identity
and inclusion and history.

00:01:06.065 --> 00:01:10.436
So it's really,
it's a story for the times.

00:01:10.436 --> 00:01:12.338
I think that's
one of the reasons

00:01:12.338 --> 00:01:14.107
that people
are responding to it.

00:01:14.107 --> 00:01:16.976
But it's also a story
about a family

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:20.779
that's as insane
as anyone else's family.

00:01:20.779 --> 00:01:22.816
So if you are born of a family,

00:01:22.816 --> 00:01:24.984
you will find a place
in this film.

00:01:24.984 --> 00:01:29.354
It's those elements
that kind of give it

00:01:29.354 --> 00:01:31.924
this emotional depth,

00:01:31.924 --> 00:01:36.095
while the satire and the social
commentary on on the other side,

00:01:36.095 --> 00:01:37.597
you know, give it a buoyancy.

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:39.765
But you're able to take in these

00:01:39.765 --> 00:01:41.734
otherwise difficult
conversations

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:44.269
and difficult issues,

00:01:44.269 --> 00:01:46.105
because you're having a laugh
while you're doing it.

00:01:46.105 --> 00:01:47.940
So it's kind of
a brilliant script

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:50.944
that Cord Jefferson,
our writer-director, wrote.

00:01:50.944 --> 00:01:52.845
So it all begins with that.

00:01:52.845 --> 00:01:55.147
And it's based on a novel
by Percival Everett

00:01:55.147 --> 00:01:56.883
called Erasure,"
so check that out.

00:01:56.883 --> 00:02:00.219
-It's really unique as well.

00:02:00.219 --> 00:02:02.789
And it's funny because you say
it is about conversations,

00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:05.858
and a lot of the film
are really funny,

00:02:05.858 --> 00:02:07.493
wonderful conversation scenes.

00:02:07.493 --> 00:02:09.628
And, you know,
Sterling K. Brown has been here.

00:02:09.628 --> 00:02:12.131
Tracee Ellis Ross been here,
talking about how wonderful

00:02:12.131 --> 00:02:13.566
it was to do such good writing.

00:02:13.566 --> 00:02:14.867
It must, you know,

00:02:14.867 --> 00:02:16.835
especially when you do
these big films like

00:02:16.835 --> 00:02:18.438
the James Bond
franchise or Hunger Games,

00:02:18.438 --> 00:02:21.907
to actually just be in a room
and acting with a scene partner,

00:02:21.907 --> 00:02:24.210
where it is just about the words
and the emotion.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.279
It just must feel so --

00:02:26.279 --> 00:02:28.347
like theater, at some point.

00:02:28.347 --> 00:02:32.251
-Well, you know, I mean,
I find enjoyment

00:02:32.251 --> 00:02:34.721
and meaning in whatever I do.

00:02:34.721 --> 00:02:36.355
I try to, if I choose well.

00:02:36.355 --> 00:02:39.892
So the scale of the film
doesn't really mean

00:02:39.892 --> 00:02:43.630
that it can't have real
emotional depth

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:46.432
and real meaning
to those of us who work on it.

00:02:46.432 --> 00:02:49.302
In fact, we're as committed
as we are on any film.

00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:51.471
This film, I guess, you know,

00:02:51.471 --> 00:02:53.139
there was something about --

00:02:53.139 --> 00:02:54.574
There were a couple of things.

00:02:54.574 --> 00:02:58.111
One, that we had an opportunity
to talk about these issues

00:02:58.111 --> 00:02:59.779
that, for example,

00:02:59.779 --> 00:03:04.416
the first scene
takes place in a classroom

00:03:04.416 --> 00:03:09.889
and there's a forbidden word
on the whiteboard behind me.

00:03:09.889 --> 00:03:14.026
I'm a professor speaking to a
class about Southern literature,

00:03:14.026 --> 00:03:17.362
and it's a discussion
that's happening in classrooms

00:03:17.362 --> 00:03:18.965
all over the country.

00:03:18.965 --> 00:03:20.599
It's a discussion
that's happening

00:03:20.599 --> 00:03:23.435
in the political sphere
right now.

00:03:23.435 --> 00:03:26.773
It's a conversation that
some people don't want to have.

00:03:26.773 --> 00:03:28.341
They want to

00:03:28.341 --> 00:03:32.512
pretend that the history
behind this stuff never existed.

00:03:32.512 --> 00:03:35.214
There's another section
that may be traumatized by it,

00:03:35.214 --> 00:03:37.717
you know, and so
it's a difficult conversation,

00:03:37.717 --> 00:03:40.953
but we wanted to have these
conversations within this film.

00:03:40.953 --> 00:03:42.388
We wanted to dive in that.

00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:44.457
We thought it was important,
and timely, as I said.

00:03:44.457 --> 00:03:48.861
But also it's a story of this
family, a family, you know,

00:03:48.861 --> 00:03:51.297
that you haven't seen before
with folks like us,

00:03:51.297 --> 00:03:53.199
but as I said, looks just
like your family.

00:03:53.199 --> 00:03:54.267
-There you go.

00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:55.802
I have a question.

00:03:55.802 --> 00:03:57.904
[ Applause ]

00:03:57.904 --> 00:04:00.106
You were doing some Zooms.
-Yeah.

00:04:00.106 --> 00:04:01.473
-You know, back in the Zoom era,

00:04:01.473 --> 00:04:03.542
when everybody was doing
press during Covid,

00:04:03.542 --> 00:04:06.312
and you were often
in front of a turtle tank.

00:04:06.312 --> 00:04:09.449
-Oh, yeah. Yeah.
-So you have two turtles?

00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:11.116
-I have two turtles.
-Still with us?

00:04:11.116 --> 00:04:12.385
-Yes, yeah.
-Okay.

00:04:12.385 --> 00:04:14.053
-They're gonna probably outlive
all of us.

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:15.421
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:18.157
-They're not a good pet to get
if you want to be in it

00:04:18.157 --> 00:04:19.425
for the short run.
-Yeah.

00:04:19.425 --> 00:04:20.960
If you're doing it on a whim,

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:22.294
no, they're probably about...

00:04:22.294 --> 00:04:23.462
-Did you get them
at the same time?

00:04:23.462 --> 00:04:24.463
-Both at the same time.

00:04:24.463 --> 00:04:26.599
Actually, my daughter came home

00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:30.970
with her mom from Canal Street
with a little brown paper bag,

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:34.073
and there were two half dollar
sized turtles in there.

00:04:34.073 --> 00:04:36.609
They're now,
like, dinner plate size.

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:40.946
One is named Nancy Nippleton.
-Sure.

00:04:40.946 --> 00:04:42.448
Classic turtle name.

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:45.384
-He's the male.
-He? Okay.

00:04:45.384 --> 00:04:47.954
-You know.
And Mouse is the female.

00:04:47.954 --> 00:04:49.688
They were named. We had no idea.

00:04:49.688 --> 00:04:52.858
But when Mouse laid eggs,
it was clear

00:04:52.858 --> 00:04:55.695
that Nancy Nippleton
had fertilized them.

00:04:55.695 --> 00:04:58.364
So hey, you know, we're fluid.
We're fluid, baby.

00:04:58.364 --> 00:04:59.799
-These are modern turtles.

00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:01.200
We're living in the new era.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.669
-Yeah, don't be so uptight, bro.
Yeah.

00:05:03.669 --> 00:05:05.070
-Congrats on the film.

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:06.338
-Thank you.
-Congrats on the nomination.

00:05:06.338 --> 00:05:08.006
Thanks for being back.
-Thank you, sir.

00:05:08.006 --> 00:05:09.308
Jeffrey Wright.

00:05:09.308 --> 00:05:10.943
"American Fiction"
in theaters now.

00:05:10.943 --> 00:05:12.712
We'll be right back
with Jon Cryer, everybody.

